  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCooler temperatures with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s will \ncontinue through Monday, before a gradual warming trend brings \ntemperatures to above normal by Wednesday/Thursday as a mid to upper \nlevel ridge builds across the area. \n\nRain chances are very low today, though radar indicates a few \nshowers possible moving across the Rio Grande from Mexico through \nmidday. Any rain chances will continue to diminish this afternoon, \nwith little to no chance for rain this afternoon. Moisture will \nincrease through Monday, with PWAT values nearing 1.5-1.9 inches \nMonday night. This, combined with a shortwave expected to move to \nthe northeast across South Texas will introduce a low chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms for the Coastal Bend and Coastal Plains \nMonday through Wednesday, however, most locations will remain dry. \nBy Wednesday night/Thursday, mid-level ridging becomes dominant and \nmoisture decreases, reducing rain chances to near zero. \n\nA northeast to east wind will continue across the Gulf waters \nthrough Thursday. While winds will generally stay between 5-15 \nknots, the persistence and swell periods around 8 seconds could lead \nto minor coastal flooding during times of high tide. Guidance does \nnot indicate tidal levels reaching 2 ft MSL, but we will need to \ncontinue watching for any minor coastal flooding impacts.",
